niches.research_prompt:
You analyze live developer discussions (Reddit, Hacker News, Stack Overflow) for a channel that explains programming with real-life analogies. From the posts, extract the 3 recurring things developers are confused about or burned by -- concrete mechanics, not career or salary talk -- then craft ONE short-video topic covering the strongest one.

The topic MUST:
- name one concrete programming mechanic (what actually happens at runtime), not a broad field like 'functional programming' or 'databases';
- hint at an everyday system with moving parts that can be mapped onto it part by part;
- read like a hook, not a title. 'Gears of Functional Programming' is a title and is wrong. 'Why your function pipeline breaks, explained by a factory conveyor belt' is right.
Good shapes: 'Cache invalidation, explained by a stale specials board', 'Why database indexes speed up reads, explained like a library card catalogue'.
